  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0149\" consists of 9 tables. The main purpose of this schema is to manage various aspects of a subscription-based service. Here are the details of each table:\n1. The \"subscription\" table: This table holds information about the subscriptions of users. It has columns for the subscription id, account id, plan, status, start and end dates, stripe subscription id, and timestamps for creation and update. The account id is a foreign key referencing the \"account\" table.\n2. The \"audit_log\" table: This table logs all the actions performed by users on the system. It has columns for the user id, account id, action, details, and a timestamp for creation. The account id and user id are foreign keys referencing the \"account\" and \"user\" tables respectively.\n3. The \"account\" table: This table holds information about the accounts of users. It has columns for the account id, email, date created, stripe customer id, stripe subscription id, plan, referrer, and an active status.\n4. The \"user\" table: This table holds information about the users. It has columns for the user id, name, email, password, date created, last login, permission, push token, and account id. The account id is a foreign key referencing the \"account\" table.\n5. The \"feature\" table: This table holds information about the features of the service. It has columns for the feature id, name, description, and a timestamp for creation.\n6. The \"invite\" table: This table holds information about invites sent by users. It has columns for the invite id, email, account id, date sent, and a boolean indicating whether the invite has been used. The account id is a foreign key referencing the \"account\" table.\n7. The \"session\" table: This table holds information about user sessions. It has columns for the session id, user id, session token, creation and expiry dates. The user id is a foreign key referencing the \"user\" table.\n8. The \"plan_feature\" table: This table maps the plans to the features. It has columns for the plan and feature id. The feature id is a foreign key referencing the \"feature\" table.\n9. The \"payment\" table: This table holds information about payments made by users. It has columns for the payment id, account id, amount, currency, payment date, status, and stripe payment id. The account id is a foreign key referencing the \"account\" table.\nAll tables have primary keys that uniquely identify each record. Foreign keys are used to maintain referential integrity, ensuring that the data in the tables is consistent and accurate. The schema also includes indexes on some columns for performance optimization.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nGravity is a Software as a Service (SaaS) boilerplate designed to expedite the development of SaaS applications. It leverages Node.js for the backend and React for the frontend. The boilerplate offers a comprehensive framework that includes user authentication, subscription management, and AI integrations with ChatGPT and DALL-E, all supported by a REST API compatible with multiple databases. \n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of Gravity is to provide a robust and flexible starting point for developers to build custom SaaS solutions quickly. By incorporating essential SaaS features, the platform aims to reduce development time and effort, allowing developers to focus on their application's unique aspects.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Utilize the boilerplate for developing custom SaaS applications.\n- End Users: Benefit indirectly through the applications built using Gravity, involving user authentication and personalized services.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles, access controls, and oversee the application's functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- User Authentication: Supports user registration, login, and session management.\n- Subscription Management: Integrates with Stripe for handling subscription plans and payments.\n- Role-based Access Control: Provides granular access permissions based on user roles.\n- AI Integrations: Integrates ChatGPT and DALL-E for AI-enhanced functionalities within applications.\n- REST API: Offers a fast, efficient API for database interactions and system operations.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Code Quality: Emphasizes a well-documented, high-quality codebase for ease of use and maintenance.\n- Performance: Designed to efficiently support multiple databases and ensure quick response times.\n- Security: Ensures secure payment processing and data integrity across the system.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0149.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0149\nCREATE TABLE `subscription` (\n  `subscription_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `plan` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `stripe_subscription_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`subscription_id`),\n  KEY `idx_account_id` (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `subscription_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `audit_log` (\n  `log_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `action` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `details` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`log_id`),\n  KEY `idx_user_id` (`user_id`),\n  KEY `idx_account_id` (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `audit_log_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `audit_log_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `account` (\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `stripe_customer_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `stripe_subscription_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `plan` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `referrer` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`account_id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `idx_email` (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `permission` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `push_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  KEY `idx_account_id` (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature` (\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`feature_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invite` (\n  `invite_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `date_sent` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `is_used` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`invite_id`),\n  KEY `idx_account_id` (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invite_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `session` (\n  `session_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `session_token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `expiry_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`session_id`),\n  KEY `idx_user_id` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `session_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `plan_feature` (\n  `plan_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`plan_id`, `feature_id`),\n  KEY `idx_feature_id` (`feature_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `plan_feature_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_id`) REFERENCES `feature` (`feature_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payment` (\n  `payment_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `currency` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_date` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `stripe_payment_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`payment_id`),\n  KEY `idx_account_id` (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
